```plsql
CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE HARNESS_APP.ARCHIVE_OLD_ORDERS(
  p_cutoff_date IN DATE
)
AS
  TYPE t_rows IS TABLE OF HARNESS_APP.ORDERS%ROWTYPE;
  l_rows t_rows;

  CURSOR c_orders IS
    SELECT *
      FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
     WHERE ORDER_DATE < p_cutoff_date;
BEGIN
  IF p_cutoff_date IS NULL THEN
    RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR(-20001, 'p_cutoff_date must not be NULL');
  END IF;

  OPEN c_orders;

  LOOP
    FETCH c_orders BULK COLLECT INTO l_rows LIMIT 10000;

    EXIT WHEN l_rows.COUNT = 0;

    FORALL i IN INDICES OF l_rows
      INSERT INTO HARNESS_APP.ORDERS_ARCHIVE VALUES l_rows(i);

    FORALL i IN INDICES OF l_rows
      DELETE FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
       WHERE ORDER_ID = l_rows(i).ORDER_ID;
  END LOOP;

  CLOSE c_orders;
EXCEPTION
  WHEN OTHERS THEN
    IF c_orders%ISOPEN THEN
      CLOSE c_orders;
    END IF;
    RAISE;
END archive_old_orders;
/
```

Assumptions and notes:

- I assumed “older than a given date” means `ORDER_DATE < p_cutoff_date`. If you want the cutoff date itself included, change that predicate to `ORDER_DATE <= p_cutoff_date`.
- The procedure does not commit or roll back. The caller is responsible for committing after the procedure completes successfully.
- The cursor is opened once, so the set of rows selected into each batch is fixed at cursor open time. DML performed inside the loop does not cause the same cursor to skip or re-read rows.
- Rows are archived first, then deleted from `HARNESS_APP.ORDERS` by `ORDER_ID`, which is the primary key shown in the context.
- If an error occurs, the exception is re-raised after closing the cursor if it is still open. The caller can then decide whether to roll back or retry.
- No ordering is required for correctness because every qualifying row is archived and deleted. If you need deterministic batch boundaries for testing or reproducibility, add `ORDER BY ORDER_ID` to the cursor; `ORDER_ID` is unique, so that would fully break ties.

